# Line-ending policy.
#
# The problem this solves: Git on Windows converts LF to CRLF on checkout by
# default. A shell script with CRLF endings fails on Linux with
# `\r: not found`, and the error names a line that looks completely fine. The
# container entrypoint is exactly such a script, so the container would refuse to
# start on a machine that cloned the repository on Windows.
#
# The fix is to normalise to LF in the repository and force LF on checkout for
# everything that a Unix tool will read.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit, because these are the files where getting it wrong is not a cosmetic
# issue: shell scripts, container definitions and CI configuration.
#
# Note: no trailing comments on these lines. `.gitattributes` does not support
# them -- everything after the attribute list is parsed as more attribute names,
# and git reports "`#` is not a valid attribute name" without saying which line
# it meant.
*.sh        text eol=lf
Dockerfile  text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
Makefile    text eol=lf

# PowerShell on Windows expects CRLF.
*.ps1       text eol=crlf

# Binary files must not be touched at all.
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.gif       binary
*.ico       binary
*.pdf       binary
*.woff      binary
*.woff2     binary

# Keep generated lock files out of diffs -- they are machine-written and a merge
# conflict in one is never resolved by hand.
*.lock      -diff
